WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.540
Reflection can sometimes access nonpublic members inside the same trusted process depending on runtime, permissions, and API usage.

2
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:16.520
Private fields, internal methods, and test-only hooks often exist outside the intended public contract.

3
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:24.380
They may not have the same validation, authorization, logging, or stability guarantees as public APIs.

4
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:31.380
Accessing nonpublic members can sidestep design assumptions, validation paths, invariants, and audit points.

5
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.800
A private field may be valid only after a constructor or setter runs.

6
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.220
An internal method may assume it is called by a narrow framework path.

7
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.400
A test hook may exist only for controlled test fixtures.

8
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.800
Do not treat private member reflection as a safe production extension mechanism.

9
00:00:49.900 --> 00:01:00.300
If behavior needs to be extended, prefer an intentional interface, service registration, policy, strategy object, or public contract.

10
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.480
Those designs are easier to review and support across versions.

11
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:14.200
If nonpublic reflection is required for a framework, migration, test, or compatibility scenario, document the reason, scope, owner, and review path.

12
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:21.420
The team should know which member is accessed, why a public alternative is not used, and what will happen if the underlying implementation changes.

13
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:30.180
Security controls should be enforced by public, intentional application boundaries rather than by hoping private members stay private inside trusted code.

14
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:36.840
Privileged administrative behavior should use explicit, reviewed capabilities rather than hidden reflected members.
